  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1243 AM CST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nAn upper low moving through central Canada, with diffluent flow aloft \nassociated with the left exit region of a 140kt jet, will provide \nfor a narrow ribbon of isentropic upglide. RRFS BUFKIT profiles \nindicate this is over a very dry atmosphere,  with ceilings above 8-\n10kft and around a 10-15C dewpoint depression through most of that \ncolumn. Profiles suggest this to be mainly rain, though the near \nsurface airmass over eastern South Dakota has seen temperatures drop \ninto the 20s. Thus, we could see some sprinkles freezing on contact. \nThere is a warmer air moving in with this moisture, with \ntemperatures in the 40s back with returns across the western \nDakotas. The NBM and REFS point to central South Dakota getting at \nleast some measurable precipitation, with about a 60% chance of \nseeing greater than 0.01\". \n\nBeing in the warm sector today, 850mb temperatures increase to \naround +8 to +9C... a standard deviation above climo. Low level \nwesterlies will enhance mixing. There is some cooler temperatures \naloft behind the warm front, but only around +6C. This weak cold \nadvection may likewise enhance mixing. West of the James valley, \nclearing and enhanced mixing will likely result in an overachievement \nin temperatures. There is some uncertainty if we will clear in time \nto get that warmer air aloft to the surface in the James valley, and \npoints east will stick with the NBM. \n\nThe warm sector of this clipper system moves east, and a backdoor \nfront comes down out of Canada for Saturday. We'll be under a broad \nupper trough by this point, with a subtle trough and shortwave \npivoting down across the area late Saturday and Saturday night \nrespectively. The first trough supports a weak system well to the \nsouth, though some light precipitation may come up as far north as I-\n90. RRFS BUFKIT profiles also identify some weak instability within \nthe cold airmass under this trough, and as such we may see some \nlight showers (nothing in the NBM at this time, added some 20%). The \nsecond wave is showing up in RRFS BUFKIT profiles as a shallow layer \nof moisture (between 4-6kft), which may be slightly cool enough for \ndendritic growth. No NBM POPs with this subtle feature at this time, \nbut by its arrival I wouldn't be surprised if we need to add a \nmention of flurries.  The cooler air remains with us through Monday \nwith milder air building out west and then moving in for the \nmiddle/latter half of the work week.",
